Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Gritty contemporary live-action throughout — working-class Milwaukee, winter light, one medium for every scene including the dream.

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A cramped fifth-floor bedroom. Sagging bed, a flour-dusted work shirt hung on the doorknob, a milk crate for a nightstand. THE NARRATOR — late twenties, thick through the shoulders, dark hair cut short, pizza-parlor t-shirt — sits on the edge of the bed and talks straight to us.

(SOUND: radiator knocking, a siren crawling down Kirk Street)

THE NARRATOR
Big brother was mine. I'm not talking about that stupid book we had to read in junior high about how the world was gonna turn weird and everybody could see everything you were doing all the time. No, I'm talking about my real Big Brother. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Basement iron under buzzing tube lights. Chipped plates, a preacher curl bench. A lifter in a cutoff sweatshirt grinds out curls with a forty-pound bar.

The door swings. NICK fills it — six-foot-plus, slabbed shoulders, reddish-brown hair uncombed, a scar on his chin and another over his left eyebrow, a face like a puzzle glued back together. THE NARRATOR trails in behind him.

The lifter drops the bar on the mat and holds it up in both hands, offering it. Nick only nods. He loads a fifty-pound bar instead and settles onto the bench.

(SOUND: bar clanging on rubber, plates ringing, Nick's breath hissing through his teeth)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
When he walked in the bar, or the gym, people took notice, and man, did they move out of the way. Of course, he had to have a 50-pound bar, but that was just for warm-ups. I aint gonna curl no fifty pounds as a warm-up. That's okay though. Nick is my brother. I know he'll stand up for me no matter what.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET APARTMENTS - DAY

Years ago. Dented garbage can lids laid out for bases between the dumpsters. YOUNG NICK, twelve, reddish-brown hair in his eyes, waits his turn to kick. YOUNG NARRATOR, eight, skinny, hangs back on a stoop.

MARK CONNER, seven, runs up and boots the ball. It rises off the pavement and goes straight through old MRS. ENGEL's kitchen window.

(SOUND: hollow thump of the kick, glass shattering, sneakers scattering on gravel)

Mark freezes, white as paper. MRS. ENGEL — housecoat, curlers — leans out over the broken pane.

MRS. ENGEL
Which one of you did that?

Young Nick steps out in front of Mark.

YOUNG NICK
I did it, Mrs. Engel. I kicked it.

A week later. At the back door, Young Nick presses a fold of bills into the hand of MA — younger here, brown hair, janitor's smock, thick glasses.

(SOUND: bills crinkling, a bus grinding past the alley mouth)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I have no idea where a 12-year old came up with sixty bucks, but I was sure proud of him for that. He never told Ma that it was really Mark who did it. None of us did.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - DAY

Yellowed linoleum. Pill bottles stand in a straight line along the counter. Out the window, five floors down, a 1980 Pontiac Trans Am sits at the curb — burnt orange, yellow flames along the side, the big eagle on the hood, T-top open.

Nick shoulders in with six loaded grocery bags in one trip and sets them down. The Narrator follows with two, breathing hard from the stairs.

(SOUND: paper bags settling, a TV game show buzzer through the wall)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The thing about Nick was, he was a real-life Viking. Tall, strong people who stayed warm in the middle of winter while everybody else froze to death. When somebody died, they floated them out to sea on a raft and set them on fire. That's the way it should be. It is so cool to see fire on top of water.

Nick tips his chin at the dark hallway and the closed bedroom door.

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

THE NARRATOR
I don't know. Why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn't be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That's how old people go downhill. They start sleepin' all day, and pretty soon before you know it, they don't want to go outside, or eat, or go to the store or nothing.

The Narrator sets a can down, works up his nerve.

THE NARRATOR
Talk to your boss for me. Down at the factory. Or I could go back to school.

NICK
You don't need a degree to get a good job. All you need is to know somebody and you will get a good job someday. That's how it always happens.

Nick pulls a beer from the bag, cracks it, drinks.

NICK
What kind of shit do you want to take at school anyway? All that crap is just a bunch of nerds talking to hear themselves talk. Nobody ever learned anything in college that you can use in the real world. The real world is here — where we live. Hell, you're too old anyway.

He goes. The Narrator stands among the bags.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick is right. I am too old. Besides, I have to take care of Ma and just do the best I can with what I got. I do like reading though. I don't tell nobody that. It doesn't matter anyway.

EXT. PIZZERIA SIDEWALK - DAY

The Narrator wheels his bike up to the front door of the pizza place. A woman coming the other way catches her toe on a broken square of cement and falls against his shoulder.

SIONNA — mid-twenties, slim, short brown hair, very pale skin, light blue eyes; black short-sleeved V-neck shirt, a necklace with a small fish symbol, jeans, blue tennis shoes, a big backpack.

(SOUND: bike chain rattling, traffic, the pizzeria door jingling)

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She looks herself over, brushes off her shirt.

THE NARRATOR
I work here, I'm just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna? That's different. I never heard of that. What does that mean?

SIONNA
My mom and dad named me after an Irish goddess. She's the River Shannon.

He isn't hearing a word of it. He is looking at her eyes.

THE NARRATOR
You want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I don't eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes a number on a scrap, hands it over, walks away smooth

EXT. STEVE'S FRONT YARD - NIGHT

A dark street of small houses. A porch light off. YOUNG NICK — seventeen, already big through the shoulders, beet-red face, pox-marked cheeks, hair every direction, big boots — crosses the dead grass with a yellow can of lighter fluid.

(SOUND: crickets, the wet squirt of the can, a screen door somewhere down the block)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
One time, back when he was in high school, Nick got so pissed off at this one kid named Steve. Steve said something mean to Nick's girlfriend, and she came to him crying about it.

Young Nick squirts a ragged line of fluid across the yard, steps back, strikes a match.

The yard goes up in a low crawling wall of flame. His face is orange in it. He does not run.

(SOUND: whumph of ignition, fire crackling; a dog starts barking)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He went over to that kid's house in the middle of the night, took a can of lighter fluid, sprayed it around his front yard a little bit, then he set that crap on fire. And he got away with it too. Everybody is afraid of Nick.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - DAY

Dishes drying on a towel. Through the doorway, MRS. ENGEL — thin, gray-haired, tired brown eyes, faded housecoat — dozes in front of a game show. THE NARRATOR wipes the table.

(SOUND: game show buzzer and canned applause from the next room, a dripping faucet)

NICK fills the doorway with his car keys — huge, beet-red face, wild eyes, hair helter-skelter, work boots.

NICK
You need to work less night shifts so you can be home to watch Ma.

THE NARRATOR
Yeah.

NICK
And you have to stop worrying about owning a car. I have one and you live close enough to work to ride the bike.

Nick goes. The door shuts. The Narrator stands there with the rag in his hand.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ma needs me. She needs me to take her places, make sure she gets her medicine and her pills. She can't even be trusted to sleep alone. Nick needs me because he needs me to take care of Ma.

He looks at the scrap of paper on the fridge with Sionna's number on it.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sionna needs me. Sionna needs me? Where did I get that one? Someday I swear I'm gonna have a better job, a house of my own, a car, and hopefully a girl just like Sionna. I have to get out of here. I'm going to the library.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - STACKS - DAY

Fluorescent tubes. The Narrator pulls a yoga book off a shelf of exercise titles, thumbs a photograph of a woman folded flat over her own legs.

(SOUND: hum of fluorescent light, a book cart squeaking past, a page turning)

He drifts one aisle over. WORLD RELIGION. He slides out a paperback on Buddhism and reads standing up, one shoulder against the shelf.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It said that attaching ourselves to other people or things will make us miserable and cause suffering. That we go looking for happiness out in the world, when the way we think about things is what can make us happy.

He sits down right on the floor, back against the stacks.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It said all things are really one thing. There is no separation to begin with. Life is suffering, and the cause of suffering is the search for things. I had to talk to Sionna about it.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

The Narrator sits on the counter with the phone cord looped around his wrist, the library book open on his knee.

(SOUND: refrigerator motor kicking on, a bus hissing to a stop outside)

THE NARRATOR
I wish you would finally come over to my place. Come meet my Ma.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Are you sure? It's kind of a big deal to meet your mother.

THE NARRATOR
Hell, she probably won't even remember meeting you when she wakes up the next morning. She's so forgetful now.

He closes the book, keeps his thumb in it.

THE NARRATOR
What I really need to talk to you about is some stuff I read in a book about Buddhism.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Oh. I didn't know you were interested in Buddhism.

THE NARRATOR
Well, remember when we were talking about meditating? I think I want to try it sometime.

SIONNA (V.O.)
You've already tried it silly.

THE NARRATOR
What are you talking about?

SIONNA (V.O.)
You are meditating at the end of every yoga practice. You know, when we just lie there at the end — in Savasana, or corpse pose.

He grins at the ceiling, embarrassed and pleased.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
She was right, of course. I was ready to zone out.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Curtains half drawn. Mrs. Engel sits in the overstuffed brown chair in front of a spinning game show wheel, one hand pressed flat to her chest.

(SOUND: TV wheel clacking, studio audience cheering)

THE NARRATOR
How's it goin' Ma?

MRS. ENGEL
What? Oh, it's you.

She looks at him like she's placing a stranger.

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay Ma?

MRS. ENGEL
Oh, I don't know. It just feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest! It's so strange, the feeling I have right now. I'm a little dizzy too.

He goes to the phone on the end table and dials, watching her.

(SOUND: rotary dial clicking back, TV laughter underneath)

THE NARRATOR
This is about my mother. Dr. Carson's been seeing her ten years. I need to bring her in. Today.

INT. DR. CARSON'S EXAM ROOM - DAY

A grey sky hangs low outside the window; a siren fades somewhere across town. The Narrator sits on the edge of the exam table. DR. CARSON — sixties, glasses, white coat, chart in hand — comes in.

(SOUND: distant siren, paper crinkling on the exam table)

DR. CARSON
Hello, I'm Dr. Carson. I think I may have met you before. You're the younger son, right?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah, that's right. Uh, how's she doin' Doc?

DR. CARSON
Your mother is fine. She is settled in another room. The thing is, I want to talk to you about her blood pressure.

THE NARRATOR
Her blood pressure? What's the matter with it? She's been takin' her pills. I make sure she takes them religiously.

Dr. Carson looks him straight in the eye.

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. It's so high, I'm afraid of the risk of a stroke. My advice is that you take her directly to the emergency room and we admit her for observation.

THE NARRATOR
Whoa man. I can't believe this. Are you sure it's that bad?

DR. CARSON
Your mother has been confused, is that right? And she also experienced chest pressure this afternoon?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah, that's right. She hasn't been acting too normal lately.

The doctor writes. The Narrator studies the floor tile.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
She was always the most reasonable one in the family. Now she rambles on about people that are dead, like it just happened yesterday.

A nurse leads Mrs. Engel in, coat over her arm. Behind her NICK fills the doorway in his work jacket, face red, eyes wild.

(SOUND: the hallway door banging back on its hinge)

NICK
What the hell is this all about now?

Mrs. Engel presses her hand to her chest, calm as Sunday.

MRS. ENGEL
There's a ringing in my ears. Someone must be talking about me.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
That's right Ma. We're all talking about you.

EXT. HOSPITAL SIDEWALK - DAY

Maples along the hospital wall going red and yellow. SIONNA walks beside The Narrator — slim, short brown hair, very pale, light blue eyes, the little fish necklace, big backpack. She catches a fallen leaf off the pavement.

(SOUND: leaves scuffing underfoot, an ambulance idling at the bay)

SIONNA
The sugars get caught in the leaves. It's like the trees are shutting down for the winter. The sugars get caught in the leaves and it makes them turn spectacular colors.

He turns the leaf over in his fingers.

THE NARRATOR
Sometimes when people get caught in bad situations, they change into something better — just like leaves.

She stops walking and looks at him.

SIONNA
That's beautiful.

(SOUND: wind through the maples; the ambulance bay doors thump shut)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I never had anyone tell me that something I said was beautiful before. Her eyes sparkled like light blue diamonds. I think I loved her. I just hadn't told her yet.

EXT. BATTLEFIELD - DAY

Smoke, churned mud, total chaos. The Narrator stands in a soldier's filthy coat, a blade in his fist, standing over a body. FALLEN SOLDIER — bearded, gutted, one hand in the mud — works his mouth.

(SOUND: screaming men, steel on steel, breath rasping close to the mic)

FALLEN SOLDIER
Save her.

Blood bubbles over his lip. He stops moving. The Narrator turns, lost, exhausted.

Through the falling men, NICK comes running at him — huge, red-faced, wild-eyed, the same helter-skelter hair.

(MUSIC: low tense drone rising under the shouting)

THE NARRATOR
Nick! Nick! Thank God you're here! I thought I was going to die.

NICK
(hissing)
That you are little brother.

Nick drives an impossibly long knife into his chest. The Narrator's hands close on the blade.

(SOUND: the crowd noise drops out to a single held ring)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

The Narrator jolts awake in a vinyl chair, gasping, both hands on his own chest. Mrs. Engel dozes in the bed, an IV line taped to her arm.

(SOUND: heart monitor beeping steady, his own breath, a cart rolling past in the corridor)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick had killed me. Nick had killed me. Why would Nick want to kill me? Me. His little brother.

He wipes his face, looks at his sleeping mother a long moment.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the feeling of betrayal by my closest family member. That had to be the worst betrayal of all.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MRS. ENGEL'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Mrs. Engel tucked in under a chenille bedspread, hair down, tired brown eyes. The Narrator sets a blue plastic pill container on the nightstand and opens its lid.

(SOUND: rattle of pills into the plastic wells, a clock ticking on the dresser)

THE NARRATOR
See

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

A single bulb over a chipped enamel sink. The Narrator stands in his undershirt, washing out a water glass, the blue pill container drying on a dish towel beside him.

(SOUND: tap water running, then shut off; a radiator knocking somewhere in the building)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Two pills at night. Two in the morning. That was the whole of my job now.

He sets the glass down. His hands stay on the counter. He does not move.

(SOUND: the refrigerator motor shudders on, too loud in the small room)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I did not want to sleep. Sleeping was where my brother was waiting.

He pulls the light chain. Darkness.

EXT. BATTLEFIELD - DAY

Smoke lies flat over churned mud. The FALLEN SOLDIER sprawls in the ruts, face turned away. The Narrator kneels in the muck in his undershirt, out of place, out of time.

(SOUND: distant shellfire, a low wind dragging across open ground)

NICK stands over him — same field jacket, same close-cropped hair, the impossibly long knife loose in his hand.

THE NARRATOR
Nick. Nick, it's me.

NICK
(quiet)
I know who you are, little brother.

Nick raises the knife. The Narrator does not lift his hands this time. He watches it come.

(MUSIC: a single held string note swelling, cut dead)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MRS. ENGEL'S BEDROOM - DAY

Grey morning through a half-drawn shade. MRS. ENGEL, hair down, tired brown eyes, propped against the pillows under the chenille bedspread. The Narrator carries in tea and the blue pill container and sits on the edge of the mattress.

(SOUND: a spoon rattling in the saucer, sparrows on the window ledge)

He tips two pills into her palm. She swallows them without looking at them.

MRS. ENGEL
You were talking in your sleep.

THE NARRATOR
Was I.

MRS. ENGEL
You were calling for your brother.

The Narrator takes the empty cup from her hands. He straightens the bedspread instead of answering.

THE NARRATOR
Two more tonight. Try to sleep.

(SOUND: the shade snapping up, traffic rising from the street below)

EXT. KIRK STREET - DAY

A narrow row of brick fronts, ash cans at the curb, wash strung between fire escapes. The Narrator sits on the stoop in his shirtsleeves, elbows on his knees, watching the corner where the street bends out of sight.

(SOUND: a streetcar bell blocks away, children's voices, a dog barking behind a fence)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every man who turned that corner, I made into Nick before he got close enough to be somebody else.

A stranger passes. Not Nick. The Narrator looks down at the pavement.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was my closest family in this world. I could not decide whether I was waiting for him or watching for him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - HALL - NIGHT

The Narrator sits on the floor in the dark hallway, back against the wall, in view of his mother's half-open door. A blanket over his knees. He is not lying down.

(SOUND: Mrs. Engel's slow breathing through the doorway, a clock ticking on the dresser)

His eyes close. He forces them open. Closes again.

(SOUND: far off, under the ticking, the flat crump of shellfire that is not really there)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick had killed me. Nick would kill me again tonight. And in the morning I would put out two pills, and two more, and say nothing to anybody.

INT. SCHOOL PLAYGROUND - DAY

Lilac bushes in full bloom along the back fence of a brick grade school. Late spring, 1970s. YOUNG NARRATOR — nine, skinny, cowlick, striped tee — and BOBBY — nine, freckled, gap-toothed — are folded over at the waist, laughing, gripping their sides. JENNIFER — brown braids to the middle of her back, red bows — stands with a friend, arms crossed.

(SOUND: distant playground shrieking, wind rattling the lilacs, a jump rope slapping asphalt)

JENNIFER
You guys are in so much trouble when Mrs. Stark finds out you're making fun of her.

Jennifer and her friend march off toward the building.

YOUNG NARRATOR
You HAVE to learn some responsibility!

BOBBY
No, YOU have to learn some responsibility!

YOUNG NARRATOR
Learn some responsibility, learn some responsibility!

BOBBY ^
Learn some responsibility, learn some responsibility!

YOUNG NICK crosses the grass to them — a head taller than any boy on the yard, broad in the shoulders, dark hair in a devilish mess. Bobby goes quiet as a mouse.

YOUNG NICK
What are you two numbskulls doin'?

YOUNG NARRATOR
Oh, just the usual crap from Mrs. Stark. She thinks we need to learn some responsibility.

The last words come out in a sputter. Tears in the corners of his eyes. Young Nick slaps his shoulder.

YOUNG NICK
Little Bro, you better start listening, man. Ma is pissed at you already. Don't get sent to the principal's office.

MRS. STARK strides up from the building — heavy heels, glasses hanging from a beaded chain.

(SOUND: her heels cracking on the walk, the boys' laughter dying)

MRS. STARK
Mr. Olsen! Mr. Olsen! Would you like to explain yourself to me before I send you in to talk to Mr. Pauls?

YOUNG NICK
Um, sorry Mrs. Stark. He didn't mean anything by it. He and Bobby were just goofing around. You should see them around my house. It never ends. I'll talk to him and Ma will talk to him. He'll never act up in class again. I guarantee it.

Mrs. Stark puts on her glasses and tips her head to take Young Nick in, head to toe.

MRS. STARK
Well, Nick, I know you're a good student. It's too bad your brother here doesn't have the same level head as you. I guess I'll let it pass this time. YOU, young man, need to follow your brother's example and start paying attention.

She turns on her heels and marches back up to the school.

YOUNG NARRATOR
Nick, um, thanks man. You saved my skin.

YOUNG NICK
You two knock it off. That is the last time I'm getting involved in your stupid crap.

He walks off to three sixth-graders who crowd in, wanting the story. He spreads his hands, already telling it.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It made me love him. It made me hate him. If they only knew the kind of stuff he did.

The boys laugh at something Young Nick says. He does not look back.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like Nick lived in a bubble, and no one could touch him. It was like he was magic or something.

INT. OLSEN HOUSE - BOYS' BEDROOM - NIGHT

Bunk beds, a model airplane hanging on fishline, a bike wheel leaned against the wall. Young Narrator sits on the bottom bunk in pajamas. Young Nick fills the doorway.

(SOUND: a radio murmuring in another room, crickets through the screen)

YOUNG NICK
So what was the joke anyway?

YOUNG NARRATOR
She was telling us we needed to learn responsibility. But it wasn't that. It was the way she said it.

The boy shrugs. It isn't funny anymore. Young Nick reaches over and musses his hair.

YOUNG NICK
You're such a numbskull, man. I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

YOUNG NARRATOR
I won't, Nick. I won't.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

THE NARRATOR — thirty, lean, sandy hair, flour-dusted apron over a work shirt — stands at the front window with a rag in his hand. Beyond the glass the grass along the curb has gone green.

(SOUND: oven ticking as it heats, a radio behind the counter, traffic passing on the wet street)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The weather was getting warmer. That must have been what triggered the memory of Nick and me in grade school.

He wipes the same spot twice, half grinning. Then the grin goes out of him.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sionna hasn't met him yet. So far I have managed to keep them apart. They are both the Alpha and the Omega, the beginning and the end — of me anyway. Without both of them, who would I be?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

MRS. ENGEL — frail, thin white hair, cardigan buttoned wrong — sits in her chair, eyes on a cop show. On the side table, a pill organizer laid out by day and by hour. NICK comes in: big boots, brown suede jacket, dark hair a mess. He throws his keys into the basket by the door and bends to kiss her cheek.

(SOUND: TV sirens, keys clattering into the basket, boots on linoleum)

MRS. ENGEL
How was your work?

NICK
Oh, you know, it was okay I guess. Those assholes next to me on the line almost caught me in the goddamn drill press today.

MRS. ENGEL
Nick, do you have to swear? It's so unlike you.

NICK
Okay, okay, Ma. No, I won't swear in front of ya. Don't want to hurt your pretty ears.

She smiles her hesitant smile and turns back to the television. Nick drops onto the arm of the couch beside the Narrator.

NICK
So, what's up with you?

THE NARRATOR
Ah, not much. Just working. And thinking about going to school.

Nick stops with the glass halfway to his mouth.

NICK
Going to school? How the hell are you going to pay for that? And what for?

THE NARRATOR
Community college. A business degree. Sionna told me I could qualify for a low-interest student loan. I checked it all out, applied, and they gave me the money.

NICK
Whoa, whoa, whoa, dude. You have really done it now. I can't believe you are letting this chick tell you what to do. It's not like she's gonna be around to help pay this back. That's all on your head, man. It sure ain't gonna be me helping your sorry ass out when you finish — IF you finish.

THE NARRATOR
This was my idea. And it would really mean a lot to me if you could just step up and be on my side for once. You are my brother, man.

NICK
Be on your side? Be on your side? Man, do you actually remember your own life? How many times have I saved your ass? Can you keep count?

Mrs. Engel looks up, forehead creased, and shakes her head slowly, side to side. Then she folds her hands in her lap and returns to the screen.

(SOUND: canned TV laughter swelling under the argument)

NICK
You remember Donny Amstel pushing you around in the locker room? Calling here and hanging up?

He leans in, dropping his voice so his mother can't hear.

NICK
I followed him home from school. When he was going in, I shoved my body in his front door so he couldn't close it. Then I told him if he ever threatened you again he would be killed. I told him our family had mafia connections. And he believed me!

Nick giggles like a little kid. Despite himself, the Narrator snickers.

THE NARRATOR
It did work, Nick. It did.

Nick hits him lightly on the arm.

NICK
You're not just my brother, man. You're my best friend. Just remember this, Bro — nothin', I mean nothing is ever gonna come between us. Not school, not Ma, and not this girl either. Bro's before ho's. You know what I mean?

A thin, greasy smile. The Narrator keeps his face perfectly still.

THE NARRATOR
Sure, Nick. Sure. I get you, man.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A boxy beige computer on a card table, textbooks stacked beside it, a spiral notebook open. SIONNA — long brown hair, pale blue eyes, plain sweater — sits beside the Narrator with a library book. MRS. ENGEL appears in the doorway, staring at the machine.

(SOUND: computer fan whirring, the television still going down the hall)

MRS. ENGEL
What's going on? What is that thing? Why do you need that?

THE NARRATOR
For school, Ma. For writing my papers.

She frowns at it a moment longer, then drifts away down the hall. Sionna slides the book across to him.

SIONNA
Learning is like meditating. You have to focus on one thing, and that thing only. If you do this, you will never forget.

She taps her own temple.

SIONNA
The ancient Tibetans pretended to see a big library in their minds. Every time they learned a fact, they walked to the section it belonged in, opened the book labeled that thing, and stored it there.

He looks at the blank screen, then closes his eyes, picturing it.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
So far I didn't have too many books, or sections of the library either. But someday, I hoped to have a library full of facts.

EXT. KIRK STREET - DAY

Blue sky, ash cans at the curb, wash strung between fire escapes. The Narrator and Sionna come out of the building door. On the corner, outside the hardware store, three teenagers ride skateboards. One looks at the couple, turns to the others, and they start laughing.

(SOUND: skateboard wheels grinding on concrete, boys' braying laughter, a bus pulling away)

THE NARRATOR
What the hell is so funny about us?

SIONNA
Nothing, unless you're thirteen. Thirteen year olds think everything is funny.

THE NARRATOR
I feel like I want to go over there and punch that kid in the face, and you're brushing it off like it's nothing. Doesn't that piss you off?

SIONNA
No. Only when we become masters of ourselves can we really deal with the world. You aren't stupid, or violent, or useless. You have a purpose.

They walk. Her hand finds his.

SIONNA
That is the meaning of loving kindness. To open our hearts and accept everyone, even those kids on the corner laughing at us. It might take my whole life, but I am bound and determined to find that inside of me and bring it out.

THE NARRATOR
Let me know

EXT. LAKESHORE - DREAM

Vibrant greens, blues and yellows. Clear blue water laps a stony shore. SIONNA — long brown hair loose, wide sleeves — walks effortlessly through the shallows toward THE NARRATOR, who holds his arms out to her. They embrace.

(MUSIC: warm strings swelling over still water)

The air goes dead still.

(SOUND: earth-shaking footsteps, heavy chains clanging and rattling)

NICK emerges from the woods as a giant — large teeth, eyes of fire, whirling a chained mace above his head. The Narrator tries to step in front of Sionna. She spreads her arms, sleeves blowing out, until she seems to be flying.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The two forces fought against each other — good against evil. Water against fire.

Nick strains. The mace will not follow the command of his body. His face goes angry and wild. He shrinks to a normal size, and the wind stops, and he is gone into thin air.

Sionna turns and smiles. The Narrator strokes her hair, kisses her face. Real lips. Real hair.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

The kitchen clock reads three a.m. The Narrator and Sionna lie folded together on a lumpy chocolate-colored couch, still in their clothes.

(SOUND: kitchen clock ticking, a lone car passing outside)

The Narrator bolts upright, almost slamming Sionna in the jaw with his head.

THE NARRATOR
What the—?

SIONNA
Oh my gosh! What's going on? We must have drifted off to sleep.

She rubs the sleep out of her eyes.

THE NARRATOR
I had the craziest dream. You were an angel, and came out of the water. You protected us from Nick with some kind of magical force!

SIONNA
(giggling)
Well, that sounds about right.

She closes her eyes again, sleepily.

SIONNA
Let's go to bed and get some sleep. We've had an interesting day.

THE NARRATOR
You can say that again.

They shuffle down the dark hallway together.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

MA sits in her chair, little afghan around her, arms folded in her lap, the squawk-box murmuring blue light on her face. Sionna sets down a casserole dish — butternut squash, cheese, crunchy nuts. On the table beside the Narrator: textbooks, and a plastic pill container he is filling slot by slot.

(SOUND: television squawking, a serving spoon scraping ceramic)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The next months seemed to drag on, with school, work, Ma, Sionna, and all of my homework pulling the last of my energy.

MA
Where's Nick? He never comes around anymore.

THE NARRATOR
He's got a poker game tonight, Ma. Or the gym. He's busy.

Ma nods and settles back. Sionna clicks off the television, sits on the arm of the chair and reads aloud from a library book. Ma listens carefully.

(SOUND: pages turning, a radiator knocking)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Within an hour she would forget everything we read. It didn't matter. She remembered who said it, and the feeling behind it.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - DAY

The Narrator hunches over a notebook on the bed, a philosophy text open beside him. A crack runs from the ceiling light to the wall. A doorbell buzzes.

(SOUND: doorbell buzzing, a bus hissing at the curb below)

Sionna comes in and flops down on the bed with an exhausted sigh — hair pinned up on top of her head, sandals, a flowered shirt going gently up and down as she breathes.

SIONNA
Geez, what a day. I have been running around like a chicken with its head cut off.

She talks. He watches her mouth move.

THE NARRATOR
I love you.

SIONNA
(squinting suspiciously)
Have you been listening to anything I said?

THE NARRATOR
Every word.

SIONNA
So, what's your paper about?

THE NARRATOR
Just some thoughts on dualism and Descartes. I think, therefore I am.

SIONNA
Perception is so cool to think about. Like, is this bed here when you don't see it? Do I exist if you don't see me? If I don't exist when you aren't seeing me, does it matter what I do?

THE NARRATOR
Don't fuck with my head. I can't take it anymore.

She cradles his head in her hands.

SIONNA
You know my parents were hippies, right? My mom always said she got pregnant with me at Woodstock. My dad went by "Mushroom," my mom went by "Jasmine." I mean, how do you think I got the name Sionna?

THE NARRATOR
I always wondered. I've never heard that name before.

SIONNA
Sionna is a Celtic Goddess — really it's pronounced like "Shanna," but no one would get that. She was the Matron of the river Shannon. The possessor of wisdom, because the river was one of the seven rivers of knowledge.

THE NARRATOR
I was always under Nick's watch. It was okay for him to do anything, but catch me doing anything, and watch out. Can you get some pot? We could hang out at your house and get really stoned and see what happens.

SIONNA
I know lots of people at school that can hook me up. It's not a big deal really. Okay hun, I will do your dirty work. I have study group in a half hour.

She kisses him and goes. He stares at the crack in the ceiling.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
My heart sank. I hated saying goodbye to her. Back to the old grind.

EXT. DINER SIDEWALK - NIGHT

Neon buzzes in a small restaurant window. Across the street, the park is dark. The Narrator comes out with a large black coffee to go and turns left. KEVIN MCCLEARY blocks the sidewalk — hulk arms, short hair gelled and parted crisply, quads pulling tight on his pant legs.

(SOUND: door bell jingling, traffic hiss, plastic lid snapping on)

KEVIN MCCLEARY
Hey man, what's up? Haven't seen you much down at the gym. Man, I heard what happened at Joe's. They said you hauled off and clocked Nick. Said you had some pretty big balls. Standin' up to Nick? That is the shit. People at the gym were all talkin' about it. Is it true?

THE NARRATOR
It totally isn't the truth. Nick almost kicked my ass, so don't think I'm a hero or anything. I just tried to stop him. That's all. Please — tell everybody it wasn't like that. He's just that same old Nick, know what I mean?

KEVIN MCCLEARY
Sure, sure, man. But I'm just sayin', it's all over town. People are saying that Nick's little brother is a badass.

THE NARRATOR
Don't believe everything you hear.

He slaps Kevin on the back and walks. His hand tightens on the coffee cup.

(SOUND: his footsteps quickening on wet concrete)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Most of the time, I would be glad to be the hero of any story. Just not one that involved Nick.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Spring light on the chocolate couch. A little red oriental box lies open on the table. Sionna lifts out a four-inch blue ceramic pipe with black swirly designs and pinches pot into the bowl.

(SOUND: bulldog next door bark-grunting through the fence, a lighter flicking, leaves popping in the bowl)

She inhales, holds it, then coughs the smoke out in little puffs.

SIONNA
Wow! That is pretty strong. I don't think I'll need much of that. Here, you go.

He inhales. The smoke rushes back out of him in a lurch. He laughs and coughs.

THE NARRATOR
Yowsa! You're right. I don't need much of that either.

She puts a CD in the stereo and sits back beside him.

(MUSIC: Green Day guitar and drums out of the small speakers)

THE NARRATOR
What do you think this song means?

SIONNA
I think it just means that none of us knows what will happen in life, but we have to make the best of things. In the end, I guess we'll see that everything is alright.

She twirls her brown hair around one finger.

THE NARRATOR
I think I'm stoned.

SIONNA
Sometimes I can see people's auras. We're made of energy, right? Energy fields surround our bodies. Different people emit different colors.

THE NARRATOR
What color am I?

SIONNA
Once or twice I saw it. You are a beautiful color of blue, like light blue. Blue is peaceful and soothing. It's loyalty, I suppose. You're a loyal person.

THE NARRATOR
You think so?

SIONNA
All you have to do is realize that you have been loyal to your Ma and your brother your entire life. I can't see my own. But I feel like I'm purplish. Faith. Mystical qualities of some sort.

THE NARRATOR
You have mystified me. Let's go outside, it's too nice to sit here.

She slings her cotton purse over her left shoulder.

SIONNA
Ice cream! We must have ice cream!

EXT. DAIRYLAND STAND - DAY

A soft serve stand on the corner. Curved cement benches around a sticky white table. Grey houses down the block, rusted cars at the curb, a kid rolling past on a skateboard. The Narrator works on a chocolate cone; Sionna's eyes are a little red. BOB stands over them, hands in his pockets, flannel shirt untucked over a small beer belly.

(SOUND: skateboard wheels, a Marlboro lighter clicking)

BOB
Wow, man! How are you? Haven't seen you since the big brawl at Joe's.

THE NARRATOR
Oh, hey man. Bob, this is my girlfriend Sionna. Sionna, this is Bob.

SIONNA
Nice to meet you.

BOB
Nice to meet you too. Did you know your boyfriend is famous? Your boyfriend here single-handedly conquered the BEAST, man. You moved like Bruce Lee, dog! I never seen Nick that pissed off in his life. Everybody is talking about it.

THE NARRATOR
Knock it off, Bob. I didn't do anything anyone else wouldn't have done. I just didn't want Nick to beat some poor guy's ass over nothing.

BOB
Oh dude, you're a hero. Everybody is bad-mouthing Nick. Sayin' he's not the same Nick as he used to be. All that shit.

THE NARRATOR
Please stop those stupid rumors, man. Nick is still Nick. I'm just me. Enough said.

BOB
'Nuff said is right. Tell Nick I want to go hang sometime.

THE NARRATOR
I haven't seen Nick. You mean, you haven't seen him either?

BOB
No, man. Not since that night. I thought he'd be at your Ma's, beggin' to the new king of the shit hill. Ha! Just kiddin' with ya, bro.

Bob walks off down the street, flicking his ashes out at the traffic.

(SOUND: cars passing, ice cream cone crunching)

SIONNA
(laughing)
Wow. You have some pretty interesting friends.

THE NARRATOR
He's NOT my friend. He's Nick's friend. I just got stuck hanging out with him.

The Narrator stares after Bob, the cone forgotten in his hand.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He hadn't talked to his friends, or been at the gym. It was time to end this feud and find him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN AND LIVING ROOM - MORNING

Smoke curls off the burner. The old coffeepot has boiled dry, the metal blackening. The Narrator, half asleep, kills the flame, takes the handle with an oven mitt and drops the pot into the wet sink.

(SOUND: metal hissing in the sink, television murmuring in the next room)

MA (O.S.)
I smell something burning, can you smell it?

THE NARRATOR
Ma, how many times do I have to tell you NOT to try to cook anything? You're going to burn the damn building down!

He comes into the living room. Ma is in her chair, afghan across her knees, already crying.

(SOUND: her breath hitching, the wall clock ticking)

He kneels and takes her hands in his.

THE NARRATOR
Now, now, Ma. Come on, don't cry. It's not that big of a deal, really.

MA
I can't remember anything anymore. I'm no good for anything.

THE NARRATOR
You're great, Ma. I love you.

MA
Nick doesn't love me. He never comes to see me anymore. What's wrong with him? Did I do something to make him mad?

THE NARRATOR
No, of course not. I tell you what — I'll go check on him. I'll make sure everything is okay.

MA
Just make sure Nick is alright. My heart is a mess already. Somedays I swear it's going to pound right out of my chest.

He holds her hands a moment longer, then looks at the clock.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Everything was blurring together.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT BUILDING - HALLWAY - DAY

Twelve-foot ceilings, an ornate carved railing, an original glass fixture throwing dusty light. The Narrator taps lightly at the door.

(SOUND: knuckles on wood, a radiator knocking somewhere below)

THE NARRATOR
Nick? Nick?

He knocks louder. A faint shuffling. The old door creaks open just a slit — the tarnished gold chain still attached. NICK's crazy brown hair, unshaven, pox-marked face, thick arm braced on the jamb.

NICK
What do YOU want?

THE NARRATOR
Will you let me in, please?

NICK
What for?

THE NARRATOR
I think it's about time we talked this out. I mean, shit, Nick, Ma is worried about you.

Silence. Nick's eyes squint.

NICK
I really don't need to talk to

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Drawn shades. Newspapers, dishes, a full ashtray. NICK — crazy brown hair, unshaven, pox-marked face, thick arms — drops onto the sagging couch. THE NARRATOR sits on the edge of a kitchen chair, hands folded.

(SOUND: a box fan grinding, traffic four floors below)

Nick aims a finger at him.

NICK
Well, let me tell you something. You... YOU... are not the brother I knew, man. You're not. All I know is now you're some hot shit college kid with a hoity-toity girlfriend, and you know everything. Everything!

THE NARRATOR
Nick, I'm not like that. I'm not. You just need to come over and see Ma and me more often. If you met Sionna, I know you would like her. Yes, she is smart. Yes, I am trying to dig my way out of my stupid job. But that's all, man. That's all there is to it.

He hangs his head, studying his own hands.

THE NARRATOR
I don't know everything. I hardly know anything. That's the thing about going to school; you find out how much there is out there that you don't know. That's where I am. I'm working my ass off, and finding out how stupid I am. That's it, man.

Nick sits back into the cushions and says nothing. A long, deafening silence.

(SOUND: a clock ticking, the refrigerator kicking on)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I felt like a man on trial, waiting to hear if I had life without parole, or the death sentence.

Nick sighs hugely. His eyes bore in.

NICK
I don't really give a shit how hard you're working. I don't give two shits about your girlfriend, and I don't give three shits about how dumb you are. But here is what I have to say. You are my little brother. I do care about you and Ma, and I will let it slide this time. But don't you ever, EVER, interrupt me in public again. You got that?

He stands, towering over the chair. The floorboards groan.

NICK
I said, you got that?

THE NARRATOR
Yes, Nick. I got that. I will try to rectify the situation with your friends. I mean, really — do they think I could actually stop you? That's so dumb. It's just a stupid made up story.

NICK
It is a stupid made up story. And yes, you will stop it. And yes, you could never stop me from doing what I want in a million years.

THE NARRATOR
So, will you see Ma? She's dyin' to see you, man.

NICK
Yeah, I'll come over there tomorrow. Tell her I've been sick or somethin'. Don't tell her about any of this shit. She doesn't know about any of this shit, right?

THE NARRATOR
Of course not. You know Ma. Hell, she cried today because she forgot the burner was on in the kitchen. She's losing her memory big-time.

NICK
I know, I know. I'm just tellin' you, man, you have to make sure you're takin' care of her. She can't be by herself, so you better be giving her her meds, or else she's going to end up in the damn hospital.

THE NARRATOR
Absolutely. Thanks, Nick. We'll see you tomorrow then?

NICK
Yeah, yeah, see you tomorrow.

Nick pulls the door open. The Narrator steps out. The door shuts behind him.

(SOUND: chain rattling home, papers and dishes being shoved around inside)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I hoped he was picking up. Maybe he was just rearranging the mess to lay down.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN AND LIVING ROOM - EVENING

MA, small in her armchair, cardigan buttoned wrong, is laughing — deep, gentle, heartfelt. SIONNA sits on the arm of the couch, jeans, sleeves shoved back, that unhurried calm.

(SOUND: Ma's laugh, the television murmuring low)

MA
On Halloween, we would watch from the bushes, and my brothers would tip those outhouses over!

SIONNA
Sounds like you were a bunch of troublemakers.

MA
We were just plain hoodlums! But I didn't have a care in the world. My parents were good people, hardworking, and regular members of the church.

She goes still. She looks at The Narrator like a child looking at a parent.

MA
Why don't we go to church anymore?

THE NARRATOR
You always say you don't want to, Ma. Do you want to? I'll take you if you want to.

MA
Yes, I do. That would be fine. Let's go next Sunday. It's been too long.

THE NARRATOR
Okay, Ma, we'll go.

He snickers under his breath at something only he can see.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
If Nick stepped foot into the church, I'm sure a bolt of lightning would hit the place.

SIONNA
What's so funny?

THE NARRATOR
Oh nothin', just thinking about church and Nick, and the combination.

Sionna carries in two plates of warmed peach pie, vanilla ice cream sliding off the crust. A knock at the door — then the door opens by itself. Nick walks in.

(SOUND: the door swinging wide, heavy boots on linoleum)

THE NARRATOR
Oh, hey, um, hi. How are you doing?

MA
Oh my God! It's you, Nick! I thought we would never see you again. Where have you been hiding?

She leans forward for her kiss on the cheek.

NICK
Oh, you know, just busy working and crap like that. How ya doin', Ma? This one been treatin' you all right?

MA
Silly you, of course, of course. We are all just fine.

Sionna sets the plates down, wipes her hands on her jeans, and holds out her hand. Nick looks at it as if it were attached to an alien being.

SIONNA
I'm Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you, I've heard so much about you.

NICK
Uh, yea, likewise.

He shakes it, reluctantly.

SIONNA
You're just in time for pie.

NICK
No thanks. I can't eat that crap. Sugar makes me feel bloated and sick.

SIONNA
Alright, that's fine. Let me know if you want anything. Don't want to make you feel left out.

NICK
Right.

Silence. Forks scrape lightly on plates.

(SOUND: forks on china, the clock in the hall)

THE NARRATOR
This is really good, Nick... are you sure you don't want some?

NICK
No, man. I'm sure.

He looks Sionna up and down, deciding what to make of her.

NICK
So, I hear you're a university lady.

SIONNA
That's right.

NICK
What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
Well, that's a tough one. I have a major in English, and will have a minor in Philosophy, so I am thinking college professor, if I can get my student teaching done and write a decent thesis.

Nick nods slowly. A heavy sigh.

NICK
Well, professor, I hope the best for ya. It's tough out there, and, like I was tellin' my kid brother here, sometimes all that school just doesn't pay off. So, I hope you can break through that glass ceiling, or whatever. Because, you know, it's even harder for a woman to teach at that level, or so I've heard.

Sionna sets her plate lightly on the table.

SIONNA
Well, Nick, I have to disagree with you there. In the last forty years, bachelor's degrees earned by female students have risen from about thirty-five to fifty-five percent. There are more females in college today than males. We make up about sixty percent of all undergraduate students. I just don't see how the future of education cannot be significantly changed for the better by the presence of more female professors. You can't keep a good woman down, so to speak.

She smiles, picks up her plate, and rises to take Ma's.

MA
Oh my.

NICK
Well, if you ask me, that might be one of the reasons this country is heading down the flippin' tubes. The women in this country can't stay home and take care of their kids.

He glances at Ma.

NICK
No disrespect, Ma. I know you had no choice.

SIONNA
You might be right in saying children need the guidance of a mother figure. And you may be right that many women today have no choice in the matter. Once you figure in the need for a dual income family just to make ends meet, women cannot afford to stay at home and "take care of kids," as you say. But I have to disagree that this is purely the reason for a failing society. Women bring different ways of thinking to the table. Diversity helps us learn. After all, we are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

She walks into the kitchen. Nick sits stunned, jaw working.

(SOUND: water running in the kitchen sink)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Never before had someone out-worded him. She had verbally beat the shit out of him.

THE NARRATOR
Well. Who would have known all that stuff? Gee, Nick, I'm really glad you came to check on Ma. Aren't you glad, Ma?

MA
Oh, yes, yes, I've missed you, dear. See, the kids have put all my pills into these little containers so I won't get confused. Isn't that right, dear?

THE NARRATOR
Yea, right, Ma.

MA
Thanks to Sionna, everything is straightening out nicely.

She folds her hands in her lap. Nick's neck goes red. He stands and pecks her cheek.

NICK
Well, I guess I better get going. Someone in this room has a job they have to go to tomorrow.

His smile is thin and snake-like.

NICK
Sounds like you two have it all figured out, with help of course from Doctor Sionna.

Sionna comes back in, drying her hands.

SIONNA
Oh gosh, are you leaving already? We hardly had a chance to talk at all.

NICK
Um, yeah. Well, I'm sure I'll see you around. Take care of this numbskull.

He grabs The Narrator's shoulders and jiggles him. His face is red, the wild hair dancing around like blazes. Sionna's face stays a still, calm pool of water.

At the threshold, Nick takes a light hold of his brother's shirt and pulls his face in close.

NICK
(low, almost a whisper)
Take care of Ma.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, you know I will.

Nick goes down the hallway, hand already shoving into his pocket for a cigarette.

(SOUND: elevator doors rolling shut, a lighter scraping behind them)

INT. UNIVERSITY LIBRARY - READING ROOM - NIGHT

Green-shaded lamps. Books spread open on a long table — the Peloponnesian Wars, a plate of Athena in helmet and spear. The Narrator's pencil stops moving.

(SOUND: pages turning, a book cart squeaking in a far aisle)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
So, just like A Tale of Two Cities, it was the best of times, and it was the worst of times. I was keeping a low profile. She never spoke about him, but he always had a snide remark about her.

He turns the page to Athena. He looks at her a long moment.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Athena. Goddess of intelligence and skill. A peaceful warrior born fully armored and ready for a battle. Not that Sionna looked for battles. She was just no one to reckon with.

He flips forward. A darker engraving. He taps it with the pencil.

(SOUND: the pencil tapping, a chair scraping somewhere behind him)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was more like this god named Menoetius. Hubristic, they called him. Exceedingly prideful and impetuous to the very end. Was I in the middle of some sort of tragic Greek play?

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Sun through the window. Sionna types at the computer, a mug of tea going cold beside her. The Narrator sprawls on the floor, shoes off, for once with nowhere to be.

(SOUND: keyboard clatter, wind chimes out on the porch)

SIONNA
Creation stories. Every religion has one. The more you look into some things, the less you believe any of it can be true.

THE NARRATOR
Not that long ago man had a hard time grasping that the earth was round. Now it seems so stupid. What if what we believe right now seems ludicrous in another hundred years?

She turns from the screen.

SIONNA
Maybe God is just energy. The energy is in all of us, and everything. It existed before time as we know it, and will go on forever.

He stares at the ceiling.

(SOUND: a floor fan turning, a dog barking two yards over)

THE NARRATOR
Not that I can even wrap my head around "forever." Does energy give a flying rat's ass if I live or die? I don't know. But down deep inside I think that if we all put our energy into something, like prayer, it has to work. Energy has power. All together, it has to increase that power.

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - CURB - DAY

Saturday afternoon. Nick's Pontiac idles at the curb. Ma comes out on The Narrator's arm in her good coat, purse clamped in both hands, still fussing at her collar.

(SOUND: the Pontiac's engine loping, a church bell tolling four streets away)

NICK
St. Patrick's. Four o'clock. I'll have her back for supper.

MA
What should I wear?

THE NARRATOR
Just wear anything, Ma. God sees you all the time anyway.

She buys it. Nick opens the passenger door and settles her in, one hand on the crown of her head so she won't knock it.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick, at church. I had to pick my jaw up off my chest.

The Pontiac pulls away. The Narrator stands alone on the curb, watching it to the corner.

(SOUND: the car accelerating, exhaust rattle fading)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
You don't just go from tough guy, leader of the pack, scary mother, to pet your Mom's head and escort her to church. He is our fearless leader, our warrior, our Viking hero. He is also one mean son of a bitch. And down deep inside, he is "us." What would I be, without Nick?

INT. SIONNA'S TOYOTA - COUNTRY HIGHWAY - DAY

The old hatchback rolls up and down the soft hills of Eastern Wisconsin. Bright blue sky, a few cumulus clouds, pine and maple in full green. Sionna drives with one easy hand; The Narrator has his elbow out the window.

(SOUND: tires humming on warm asphalt, wind through the open window)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
First week of summer break. Ma was in the hands of Nick the chameleon, and I was actually taking a break for once.

THE NARRATOR
They actually like being called Mushroom and Ginger? You're sure?

SIONNA
Yep. Just call them Mushroom and Ginger. They have gone by that since the sixties. They wouldn't even respond to Robert and Margery.

A deer flickers among the trees at the shoulder and is gone.

THE NARRATOR
Oh, so they do have real names.

SIONNA
Yes. They were raised right here in the good 'ol Midwest, christened with proper names and everything!

THE NARRATOR
I can't imagine they have great relationships with their own parents?

SIONNA
My father's parents had five other kids, so I think they just mentally divorced him. I remember seeing them once at the farm. It was a bad scene, a lot of yelling and my grandmother crying. Her hair piled up in a beehive, a cheap looking blue polyester dress. She looked at me through the screen door, tears streaming down her face. They're both dead now.

She keeps her eyes on the road, moving the wheel in a sleepy manner.

(SOUND: gravel ticking under the tires as she drifts to the shoulder and back)

SIONNA
Mom's parents tried really hard to be in our lives when I was little. But Grandma and Grandpa just didn't get tofu. They didn't get making yogurt, unpasteurized milk, drumming around an outside fire, you know.

She starts to chuckle.

SIONNA
They also discouraged any belief in the tooth fairy, the Easter bunny, or Santa Claus. Elves — that was possible. Wood nymphs, the spiritual life of plants, trees, and water — all okay. I guess you could say I never got my hopes up unnecessarily.

THE NARRATOR
So, your parents are pagans.

She looks over at him and decides.

SIONNA
Yea. I guess that's as close as we're going to get.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Textbooks and highlighters spread across the coffee table. An open bag of chips between them. SIONNA sits cross-legged in a wool sweater, brown hair loose. THE NARRATOR lies on the floor with an accounting text propped on his chest.

(SOUND: radiator knocking, a spoon ticking against a mug)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This is Murphy's Law. Whatever you think is going to happen is not what happens. Basically, it's pure irony. Just when you think everything is going your way, man, suddenly, it isn't.

Sionna nudges the chip bag toward him with her foot.

SIONNA
Wow, your brother is sure suddenly into taking care of your mother, isn't he?

THE NARRATOR
I can hardly remember the last time I made dinner over there. He always is taking her out or bringing something over for her. I don't know what's gotten into him. Does seem kind of weird, doesn't it?

He stares at the ceiling.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It wasn't that I felt like I was neglecting her. It was a weight off my shoulders. I had been the caretaker for so long.

THE NARRATOR
He'll be back to neglecting her in no time. Then I'll have to start hanging out there more. I'm sorry for all this. I know it's a drag.

Sionna looks upset. She reaches over and pats the side of his head.

SIONNA
Oh no, no, it's not like that at all. I can only hope I have a son or daughter like you to take care of me when I'm old. Isn't that one of the reasons we have children? To help us in our golden years?

THE NARRATOR
Well, I suppose. It just feels kind of like we're indentured servants or something.

SIONNA
(laughing)
Oh my god, you're reading too much. I think of it as part of the circle of life. First they feed us and change our diapers. They teach us everything we know. Then we're all grown up and at the same level. Then finally they start to trend downwards and we're helping them eat and possibly changing diapers.

Her eyes go far away. She picks at the label of a candle jar.

THE NARRATOR
Right, well, I don't have anyone around yet to change my diapers. Unless you'd like to sign up, of course.

SIONNA
No, that's okay.

THE NARRATOR
Do you want to have children someday?

SIONNA
To be honest, I'm too busy right now to think about it. I'm going on 30 though, so I guess I better get my shit together if I'm going to. I really want to be ready. I want to honestly have lots of time to devote to taking care of another human. I think that's the least I can do.

THE NARRATOR
I agree. Look at all the people that don't have time, and the kids they end up with.

SIONNA
What about you? Do you want kids?

THE NARRATOR
I suppose. I do need someone to change those diapers.

She swats his arm. He turns off the lamp.

(SOUND: lamp switch clicks, furnace settling)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a great night. It was a normal night. It was the last normal night.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Nine in the morning. September light on a dead television. MA's chair sits empty, the afghan folded over one arm. No purse on the hook. No coat. The kitchen pill bottles stand in a neat row, untouched.

(SOUND: a clock ticking in an empty room; a bus sighing five floors below)

The answering machine's red light blinks. THE NARRATOR presses the button.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olsen, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

The tape whirs to the next message.

NURSE OWENS (V.O.)
Hello Mr. Olsen, this is Patricia Owens, one of the nurses at Frodo Hospital, with an update on your family members.

He copies the number onto the back of an envelope, dials, hunches over the phone, rubbing his temple.

THE NARRATOR
What's going on? Is Ma okay? I wasn't here last night. I have no idea where she is.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
We know, Mr. Olsen. Everything will be okay. We just think it best if you came in to talk directly to us.

He drops the receiver in the cradle and is out the door.

EXT. POLICE STATION - CENTRAL AND 3RD - DAY

He pedals hard down the sidewalk. A kid bails off a skateboard into the gravel shoulder, shouting after him. He doesn't look back.

(SOUND: bike chain whirring, the kid's shout torn away by wind)

An ancient stone building. Carved lion heads and stars over the windows. A metal plaque beside the door: 1898. He drops the bike against the stone.

INT. POLICE STATION - WAITING AREA - DAY

A chest-high counter. Scattered people waiting on wooden chairs. A brunette officer sets a styrofoam cup of water in front of him and gives him a sorry look.

(SOUND: radio dispatch crackle, phones ringing behind the counter)

OFFICER DUPUIS enters — big, graying hair, thin mustache. He pulls a chair around, sits close.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Thanks for coming down to the station, Mr. Olsen. It seems that there has been a serious accident involving your brother, Mr. Nicholas Olsen, and your mother, a Mrs. Olivia Olsen. Last night about eight p.m. they were hit broadside going through the intersection of Werner and Lowe.

The narrator's hand tightens on the cup. Water shivers in it.

OFFICER DUPUIS
I regret to tell you that your brother is in serious condition at Frodo, and your mother passed away at the scene. Her body is at Sacred Heart Funeral Home.

(SOUND: the room's noise drops to a hollow ringing; a chair leg scrapes far away)

Dupuis keeps talking. We see the mustache moving. We hear nothing but the ring.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He continued to talk, but I have no recollection of what he said. Everything is a blur.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - ICU CORRIDOR - DAY

Scrubs moving fast in both directions. Monitors chirping out of every doorway. The air smells of urine and chemicals.

(SOUND: overlapping monitor beeps, a ventilator hiss, wheels on linoleum)

NURSE OWENS walks him to a bank of high windows and stops.

NURSE OWENS
He's unconscious for now, but stable. His right lung was punctured and his right femur is broken. They'll take him to surgery later to put a rod in it.

Through the glass: the top of NICK's head, erratic fire-colored hair poking straight up, a white collar brace framing a face we cannot see. Wires and machines crowding the bed.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
They had to pull Ma out with the jaws of life. She never knew it hit her. At least she'd just been to mass.

NURSE OWENS
Toxicology is still pending. The officers think he may have been drinking. Are you up to seeing him?

He looks at the hair above the mattress a long moment.

THE NARRATOR
No. I'm really not up to seeing him. Sorry.

He turns and walks out.

(SOUND: doors thumping shut behind him, footsteps quickening)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

He stands in front of MA's chair, coat still on. The afghan. The dent in the cushion. The kitchen doorway where she went for her pills.

(SOUND: absolute silence, then a single hard sob)

He goes down onto the floor, holding his head, pulling at his own hair. Water spots the linoleum.

THE NARRATOR
Die, you fucking jackass. Die. Die.

The apartment gives him nothing back. A key in the lock. SIONNA comes in fast, drops her bag, kneels, folds him into her chest and pats his head.

SIONNA
Just let it out. Let it out. We'll figure it all out. Don't worry.

(SOUND: rain starting against the window; the phone ringing, unanswered)

The phone keeps ringing. She reaches up and takes it off the hook.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
One day you're talking to her. The next day you're picking out the casket, the lining, the vault to keep out the creepy crawlers while she lays six feet under. One day you're talking to her, and the next day you're putting her in a box.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - GRAVESIDE - DAY

A small knot of mourners around a light maple casket at the top of a hill. A few neighbors from the apartment building, two elderly coworkers, a handful of friends. FATHER JOSEPH sprinkles holy water, murmuring the prayers.

(SOUND: wind in bare branches, a rosary clicking)

BOB hangs at the back in a borrowed sport coat, hands folded, solemn.

BOB
I've been checking on Nick all the time, man. I'll watch over his apartment, don't worry about nothing.

THE NARRATOR
Good.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Because I didn't want anything to do with that prick, even now. I would be the only son there. The oldest one would not be able to attend his own mother's funeral. Rightly so. Since he killed her.

He takes a handful of dirt and lets it go over the lid.

(SOUND: a muted thunking as the dirt hits the casket)

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - NICK'S ROOM - DAY

Five days of trauma on one face. NICK — the big frame gone slack under a white bedspread, the fire-colored hair still shooting straight up, oxygen tube in his nostrils, bruising gone black and purple to dark green around the eyes and along the right jawline. His right leg a huge limb under the blanket. A chest tube runs to a rectangular plastic box.

(SOUND: monitor beeps, the chest-tube box gurgling with each breath)

He clicks off the TV with the remote, IV taped to the back of his hand.

NICK
(weak)
Hey, bro.

THE NARRATOR
Hey.

NICK
I guess I really fucked up, man. Can't believe it. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears well in his bloodshot eyes and run down through the bruising. The narrator stands at the foot of the bed. Doesn't sit.

THE NARRATOR
They took care of it. The mass was nice. She's at peace now. The plot is at the top of the hill at St. Mary's. Flat stone, near some trees. She's okay.

NICK
I didn't mean to have three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I had to take her to church. I went anyway. I can't remember what hit us. It was all so quick.

He shifts, winces, grabs the bed rail.

NICK
The cops are after me now. They're telling me it could be vehicular manslaughter. Involuntary manslaughter of Ma. No way. It was a mistake. I was almost killed too. Why would I want to hurt Ma? I had to call a lawyer. When I get out of here, I'll have a year of legal crap. Like it was all my fault.

THE NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Dead-on stare. Nick's face turns.

NICK
Well, wait a minute. What do you mean? You think I meant to kill her? You think I meant to kill Ma? You son of a bitch.

(SOUND: the monitor alarm chirping faster; a nurse's voice out in the hall)

The narrator does not move. Nick strains up an inch and falls back into the pillows, tethered.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
All of his bullying, all of his violence, it meant nothing at this moment. He was trapped in a hospital bed with tubes sticking out of him, and he couldn't hurt me.

THE NARRATOR
Think about it, Nick.

He walks out, leaving Nick's face twisted into a mixture of rainbows and roadkill.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - GRAVESIDE - DAY

Late fall. A bicycle laid on its side in the brown grass. Low sun on a new marker: MOTHER — OLIVIA ANNE OLSEN — AUGUST 28, 1933 TO SEPTEMBER 7, 1998 — I HAVE FOUGHT THE GOOD FIGHT. I HAVE FINISHED THE RACE. I HAVE KEPT THE FAITH. 2 TIMOTHY 4:7.

(SOUND: wind over open ground, a distant crow)

He rights the inverted metal vase, sets in a few golden mums, pours water from his bottle. Droplets fall from his chin onto the thin new blades of grass.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, you did, Ma. You did keep the faith.

He crouches, thumb working at a spot of mud on the stone.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
They say grieving is a process and it never really ends. Fifty years can go by and you'll just break down all of a sudden. I hope she's finally happy. I hope she has no more work or worry. Leave that to me. I have plenty.

(SOUND: bike chain catching as he pedals away down the cemetery lane)

INT. TONY'S PIZZA PARLOR - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Friday, six o'clock. Flour dust in the light. THE NARRATOR spins dough at the bench beside the big ovens. TONY, seventy, apron over a cardigan, works the register. TOMMY hunches over a road map spread on the prep table, copying an order onto a slip.

(SOUND: oven roar, order-wheel bell, phone ringing)

THE NARRATOR
Jesus, Tommy, it's Mr. Jones. Six p.m., Friday night, half pepperoni, half mushroom, black olive, just like every other Friday since the day I set foot in this place. You do know how to get there, right?

He throws his hands up in a quizzical Godfather movement.

TOMMY
If you're so goddamn smart, you go deliver it.

THE NARRATOR
I will. And I can guarantee it will be much faster than you can.

Tony looks at him, then tosses over his car keys.

TONY
Go on. Get out of here.

He grabs his old coat and the hot box and goes out into the dark. Time cut: he comes back through the door twenty minutes later, red-cheeked, stuffing three crumpled singles in his apron, and stands close to the ovens with his hands out.

(SOUND: the door bell jangling, wind cut off, oven fans humming)

Tony hits his shoulder.

TONY
Man, that was quick. How would you like to do more deliveries for me?

THE NARRATOR
Sure. Whatever you think.

TONY
You look tired, kid. I was awful sorry about your ma.

The narrator nods, says nothing, and turns back to the dough.

INT. SIONNA'S TOYOTA - COUNTRY HIGHWAY - DAY

DREAM. Cloudless blue sky. The passenger seat and floor are stacked with Tupperware, pies, casseroles under crinkling aluminum foil. THE NARRATOR drives, pulling into one gravel driveway after another, setting food down on porch steps.

(SOUND: chickens, a dog barking, a man laughing and singing somewhere behind the wheel noise)

He looks over. SIONNA is beside him now, enormously pregnant, the round of her belly nearly pressed into the dashboard. She smiles.

The next moment she is holding a newborn wrapped in a towel. She sets it gently on the seat between them. He looks down: where the baby's face should be, there is a small pizza.

(SOUND: an infant's cry warping into the ring of an oven timer)

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - BEDROOM - NIGHT

His eyes pop open in pitch dark. Her body heat beside him. The clock radio reads 3:41.

(SOUND: her sleeping breath, sleet ticking the storm window)

SIONNA
(sleepily)
What's the matter, babe?

THE NARRATOR
Oh my god, I had a dream we were at your parents' house and you had a baby. But in place of a face, it was a pizza. What the hell does that mean?

She laughs into the pillow and squeezes his arm.

SIONNA
Well, it means you work too much. It also means you are giving birth to a new idea. Dreaming about being pregnant or having a baby means you are giving birth to new ideas. Are you having ideas about pizza?

Her breathing goes long and even. He lies there, eyes open, staring at the ceiling.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
What if we could guarantee a fast delivery? What if we could get the pizza to someone in a half hour, or they didn't have to pay?

INT. TONY'S PIZZA PARLOR - KITCHEN - DAY

A thin layer of snow on the sidewalk beyond the front window. He stands at the oven door, daydreaming, peel in hand. The door bell jangles. BOB comes in, coat open, eyes enormous.

(SOUND: door bell, wind gust, the oven's low roar)

BOB
Oh my god, look who's shaking. Little brother. Man, what is up?

THE NARRATOR
Oh, hey, Bob. Nothing, man. What's been going on with you?

BOB
Man, man, oh man. What the hell is going on with Nick? Have you talked to him? He barely survived, and now the cops are hounding the shit out of him. They might lock him up, man. Might lock him up. What's he going to do?

THE NARRATOR
Um, I don't know, man. You know the rules. He accidentally killed one person.

BOB
Oh, dude, you don't mean to tell me you're okay with that. He's your brother, man. He's Nick, man. Nick would fucking kill somebody if they locked him up.

The narrator sets the peel down hard on the bench. His face goes red.

(SOUND: metal peel clanging on steel, the phone starting to ring behind him)

THE NARRATOR
Oh, like he already did? Bob, I don't know what to tell you except I'm not okay. I'm not okay with Nick. I'm not okay with Ma being dead. I'm not okay with anything.

Bob backs away slowly, hands up.

BOB
I am so sorry, man. I am so sorry about your mother and Nick and all of it. I hope things get straightened out soon. If you need anything, you'll know where to find me.

He tries a weak half-baked smile.

THE NARRATOR
Yeah. Sorry, Bob. I am too. Thanks for stopping in. It's not your fault, man.

Bob bobs his head and goes out into the freezing air. The narrator stares into the oven door until the phone's ringing pulls him back.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

A tin of chocolate cookies open on the coffee table. He sits on the floor in front

INT. MARK JOWLISH'S LAW OFFICE - HALLWAY - DAY

A row of orange plastic chairs under a NO SMOKING sign. NICK sits with his bad leg stretched into the aisle — heavy through the shoulders, thick dark hair combed flat, a hospital discharge band still curled around his wrist. He shakes a Marlboro out of the pack and lights it under the sign.

(SOUND: fluorescent tubes buzzing, a telephone ringing behind a closed door)

NICK (V.O.)
That little bitch. It's all that little bitch's fault. She really is the reason this is all going down like this.

He blows the smoke straight up above his head.

NICK (V.O.)
I had lots and lots of time to think while I laid in that hole they call a hospital. One guy comes in and says something about my lungs. The next guy comes in and says something about my leg. What a bunch of quacks.

He picks a magazine off the table, can't bring himself to open it, drops it back.

(SOUND: the magazine slapping the table, the radiator knocking)

NICK (V.O.)
Soon as I got out, the cops started harassing me. My alcohol level was too high. My ma was the victim. What about me? I know she's dead, but what about my pain and suffering? Like it means nothing at all. Nothing.

He rubs his knee. Down the hall, an office door stays shut.

NICK (V.O.)
Bob's the only friend who's a real friend. Talked to my landlord, made sure my bills got paid. He owes me anyway. It wasn't the only time I had to pull my brother out of some problem, either.

INT. JUNIOR HIGH CAFETERIA - DAY

Years earlier. Steam trays, linoleum, three hundred kids. In the far corner, YOUNG NARRATOR — twelve, skinny, big goofy eyes, hatless, hunched over a lunch tray. TEEN NICK — fifteen, letter jacket, thick dark hair, already built like a wide receiver — crosses from the jocks' table.

(SOUND: cafeteria roar, trays clattering on steel)

TEEN NICK
Dude, what is going on?

YOUNG NARRATOR
John Johansson tried to trip me in the hallway. He stole my hat and now I think the two dollars ma gave me is gone. I can't find it. He probably stole that too.

The boy's eyes go watery.

TEEN NICK
Okay. I'll see what I can do. But I'm just telling you to stop that blubbering. You're embarrassing the shit out of me.

YOUNG NARRATOR
Okay, Nick. Okay.

Teen Nick swings back to STEVIE SUNDERMAN — freckles, buzz cut, mouth full of sandwich.

TEEN NICK
Which one of the seventh graders is John Johansson?

STEVIE SUNDERMAN
That kid over there. The one with the curly blonde hair.

Teen Nick walks at a table of small boys. JOHN JOHANSSON — curly blonde hair, black jeans, flannel shirt — turns the color of chalk.

TEEN NICK
Come over here. I need to talk to you.

Johansson follows him to the propped-open doors. Beyond, past the frozen practice field, a football goalpost.

(SOUND: the door bar clunking, wind whistling through the gap)

TEEN NICK
See that pole down there? Do you want me to hang you upside down from the top of it so you can't get down?

JOHN JOHANSSON
No. What do you mean? I didn't do anything. I swear.

Sweat stands on the boy's lip.

TEEN NICK
If you so much as talk to my brother, trip him or steal any of his stuff from now on, you're going to hang upside down from that pole until you pass out and die. You understand me?

JOHN JOHANSSON
Okay, okay. I swear I didn't do anything. I won't talk to him. Jeez.

TEEN NICK
Don't jeez me, you little asshole. Get back over there with your friends and eat your stolen lunch.

Johansson runs like a gorilla is chasing him. Teen Nick laughs into his fist.

NICK (V.O.)
He was always totally dependent on me. He still is, if you ask me. He would be lost without me. That, I am certain of.

INT. MARK JOWLISH'S LAW OFFICE - DAY

A small office: a desk buried in paper, a computer, a nameplate reading MARK JOWLISH, ATTORNEY AT LAW. MRS. HALSTEAD — sixty, old-fashioned beehive hairdo — walks Nick in at a crawl and shuts the door.

(SOUND: a wall clock ticking, the door latch clicking shut)

MRS. HALSTEAD
Mr. Olson, come this way.

MARK JOWLISH enters — greased hair, shabby suit — and puts out a hand.

MARK JOWLISH
Hello, Mr. Olson. Mark Jowlish.

They shake. Nick lowers himself into the chair, stiff-legged, and talks with his hands. Jowlish writes nothing.

NICK
Ma needed to get out and go to church. It was up to me to get her there because of my dumbass brother. Nobody knows the whole situation. Unless you really know somebody, you can't judge them.

Jowlish gives him a long, disappointed stare and squares the file.

MARK JOWLISH
For now your license is revoked. You can't drive, and you shouldn't drink — they can show up any day to test you. As for the manslaughter charge, I don't think I'll be able to represent you. You need a different kind of lawyer.

(SOUND: a chair leg scraping, papers being pushed across the desk)

NICK (V.O.)
I can't work. My leg isn't right yet. I can't drive. I've got money saved in the bank, but this is going to bleed me dry before it's all said and done.

INT. COUNTY COURTROOM - DAY

Fluorescent light, blond wood, a gallery of shuffling strangers. NICK stands at the rail in a black pinstripe suit, shoes scuffed, palms wiped on his trousers. Beside him DANIEL DAWSON — near sixty, blond, pressed suit, shined shoes. On the bench, JUDGE MARLOWE — heavy, brown hair, big glasses.

(SOUND: a gavel tapping, murmuring in the gallery, a radiator hissing)

DANIEL DAWSON
Drunk at the wheel, they'll call it vehicular manslaughter. You could be looking at up to ten years. If they keep it a misdemeanor, maybe a year in jail.

Nick's jaw works. Two teenagers in handcuffs are led out past him.

NICK (V.O.)
Drug dealers, carjackers, a bunch of stupid punks. The way I see it, there's three kinds of people. Leaders like me. Dipshits like these morons clogging up the jail system. And invisible people, like my brother.

JUDGE MARLOWE
State your name for the court.

NICK
Nick Olson.

JUDGE MARLOWE
Were you in the car with your mother?

NICK
Yes.

The judge reads the blood alcohol number aloud. Dawson talks fast and low. Nick stares at the seal on the wall.

(SOUND: pages turning, the gavel coming down once)

